<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=mercator-projection">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mercator projection</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1569">1569</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mercator projection is a way of drawing the globe on a flat map in which courses of constant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a> bearing, rhumb lines, appear as straight lines, so that a chart serves sailors directly. Gerardus Mercator introduced it on his world map of 1569, printed from 18 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates he engraved himself, by increasing the degrees of latitude towards each pole as the parallels lengthen. He left no account of his method, and Edward Wright and others later showed how to calculate the latitude scale exactly. Acceptance was slow and came only with the French sea atlas Le <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neptune">Neptune</a> Francois at the end of the seventeenth century; the projection is still the basis of navigation charts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mercator-projection.jpg" alt="Mercator projection" width="330" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerardus Mercator · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mercator_1569_world_map_composite.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Mercator projection is a conformal cylindrical map projection first presented by the Flemish geographer Gerardus Mercator in 1569. Earlier charts did not use it. The 13th-century portolan charts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, the earliest to survive, are generally not believed to rest on any deliberate projection. They carried networks of windrose lines that helped set a ship's bearing, and over so small a region a course of constant bearing was approximately straight on the chart. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-needham">Joseph Needham</a> suggested that some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> star charts used the Mercator projection, but he gave no evidence, and the historian Kazuhiko Miyajima concluded from measurement that they used an equirectangular projection.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The projection can be pictured as wrapping a cylinder around a sphere, touching it along the equator, and unfolding the sphere onto the cylinder so that angles between intersecting curves are kept. Scale is exact along the equator and grows with distance from it, as the secant of the latitude: about 1.15 at 30 degrees, 2 at 60 degrees and over 11 at 85 degrees. Areas therefore swell towards the poles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> appears about the size of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, though Africa is 14 times as large.</p><p>Its purpose was navigation. Any course of constant bearing, called a rhumb or loxodrome, appears as a straight segment, so a ship can keep one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a> direction rather than making error-prone corrections. Mercator observed that on such a course a ship would not arrive by the shortest route but would surely arrive. He understood the difference between rhumb distance and great-circle distance, and advised that rhumb measurements suit short or moderate courses, especially at low latitudes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the 18th century it became the standard projection for navigation, and practically every printed marine chart is based on it. In the late 19th and early 20th centuries it was perhaps the commonest projection for world maps, until atlases largely abandoned it for such maps in the 1940s. Critics such as George Kellaway and Irving Fisher judged it unsuitable for general world maps. It has been conjectured that it made people think of countries near the equator as less important, but later perception studies have failed to find such an effect. Mercator himself used the equal-area sinusoidal projection to show relative areas. Variants remain in use, including Web Mercator for online maps and the transverse Mercator behind most national grid systems.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mercator projection” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
